Throwaway harnesses that link the real engine sources and drive the actual
entry points. They are kept because the behaviour they cover -- clicking a
save slot, rolling back, opening and closing the game menu, switching the
help screen's panels -- is not reachable from the Makefile's targets, and
every bug found through them was in a path that read correctly.

Build one against the whole engine (about 40s). Keep this list in step with
the Makefile's OBJS; a stale one fails to link rather than misbehaving:

  SRC=$(for f in save history serial stmtid expr interp scene vfs lex parse style \
        slang slparse image png gfx audio font ui slrender mp3impl tl md5 \
        persist musicroom pyfn rhythm list config; do \
        echo -n "src/$f.c "; done)
  gcc -O0 -g -I src -I tools/hostshim -I libs -o /tmp/rbtest \
      tools/harness/rbtest.c tools/hostshim/plathost.c $SRC -lpng -lm
  cd /tmp && /tmp/rbtest "$OLDPWD/tools/harness/fixgame"

tools/hostshim/plathost.c is linked into every harness, which is why it is on
that line and not in the source list. It is plat.h -- the seam between the
engine and the machine, see src/plat.h -- for a machine with no window, no
message queue and no display. All of it does nothing; what makes it
unavoidable is image.c, which times its PNG decodes with mr_plat_ticks.

A harness must not define an mr_plat_* function of its own: the link fails on
the duplicate. One that wants to assert on a platform call -- "did the 'r' key
ask to restore the window size?" -- should drop plathost.c from its own build
line and supply the whole set.

A pak is a game. mr_vfs_init mounts the archive and returns (vfs.c:186) --
the loose directory is what it falls back to when there is no archive, not a
second place to look -- so for anything given a pak, the directory argument
decides nothing at all. run.sh takes the game from MR_PAK and from nowhere
else:

  tools/harness/run.sh                          # mrpy.pak
  MR_PAK=$PWD/azami.pak tools/harness/run.sh    # another game
  MR_PAK=/nope tools/harness/run.sh             # no game: five skip

There was an MR_GAME as well, pointing at a game folder beside the repo, and
all it could do was pair one game's script with another game's archive.
playtest reported a pass for months that way.

Arguments are not uniform, and getting them wrong looks exactly like a broken
engine rather than a broken command line -- a whole suite run once came back
with four "failures" that were only this. Most take a directory alone, mount
no archive, and get a *fixture* directory:

  /tmp/rbtest tools/harness/fixgame

These take the pak first and the game directory second, because they need
something only the archive has (translations, or the audio):

  juketest langtest tltest audiostoptest rgtest playtest unlocktest
  cuttest confirmtest slotfittest stacktest datetest durtest latencytest
  seamtest oldsavetest standintest cutskiptest

Most of them mean the *fixture* pak, not a game's. Only five still want a
real game -- cuttest, langtest, slotfittest, stacktest and seamtest -- and
any game's will do, verified against three of them; run.sh skips them when
there is no pak rather than failing.

(Those three were miwa.pak, azami.pak and an mrpy.pak that held Hinata, back
when `wmake data` wrote mrpy.pak whichever game it packed. Each game's
makefile now names its own -- mrpy.pak is Miwa, hinata.pak is Hinata -- so an
mrpy.pak predating 2026-10-18 is whatever was built last.)
Running the suite with no game installed and no pak
built is a supported state, and is how the engine is meant to be published.

What moved into the fixture to get there, and why each one could not simply
be invented:

  the jukebox and the high score table
      common/screens.rpy builds both over the fixture's own generated
      tones, named after their lengths, with the last two gated on
      persistent.unlock_a and persistent.unlock_b. juketest and unlocktest
      read them. The seven-plus-two shape is the thing unlocktest asserts.

  fixgame/script_game.rpy
      twenty say statements copied *verbatim* from Miwa, under their own
      labels chap0 and chap6. They are hash preimages, not content:
      oldsavetest's save fixtures name their resume point by statement id
      and tltest matches translation ids, and both ids are md5s of the
      reconstructed source line. Change a character -- an accent, a space,
      the speaker tag -- and both tests go red. Nothing jumps to these
      labels and nothing reads them as story.

  fixgame/tl/english/script_game.rpy
      the twenty `translate english chap6_83afebf2:` blocks Ren'Py
      generated for those lines, also verbatim. This is the point of the
      whole arrangement: the ids came from Ren'Py, so tltest checks our
      parser against Ren'Py rather than against itself. A catalogue
      generated from our own parser would agree with any drift in
      mr_tl_identifier and assert nothing.

run.sh in this directory is all of this in executable form -- it builds and
runs the lot, or the ones you name, with the right arguments and source lists.
Prefer it to retyping the commands below; the list of seven above was short by
one for a while, and the harness it omitted looked like a failing test.

These seven take a *fixture* directory instead of the game, and there are
three of those -- tools/harness/story for the ones that walk a story,
tools/harness/choice for the menu, and tools/harness/credits for a scrolling
transform that is still running when a transition starts:

  restarttest bandtest windowtest rbmusictest titlesavetest  <- story
  branchtest afmtest                                         <- story
  substtest prefixtest condtest movetest blurtest animtest   <- story
  stalltest                                                  <- story
  bartest skiptest savatltest savmenutest pausebacktest      <- story
  inputtest                                                  <- story
  choicetest                                                 <- choice
  creditstest                                                <- credits

None of the three needs the game any more. They used to symlink options.rpy,
screens.rpy, gui.rpy, fonts/ and gui/ out of ../../../game, which made the
suite depend on a directory that is not in this repository and cannot be
published with it. Those five now live once in tools/harness/common/ and the
fixtures link to them; `find tools/ -type l` should show nothing pointing
outside the tree, and that is the invariant to keep.

What is in common/, and what was deliberately left behind:

  fonts/          the baked atlases, DejaVuSans + NotoSansJP
  gui/            284KB, 41 files: the Ren'Py template's widget images --
                  bar, button, scrollbar, slider, frame, namebox, notify,
                  nvl, skip, textbox, game_menu, bubble, thoughtbubble
  *.rpy           the template's three, as the game generated them

Not copied: dosborder.png, overlay/main_menu.png, phone/, the window icons.
That is 4.1MB of the game's own art, and no harness reads any of it.

gui/ is needed, which is worth writing down because it is not obvious and
cost a round of debugging to establish. Every story-based harness passes
with gui/ deleted outright -- 26 tests, all green -- so it looks like dead
weight. choicetest is the exception: gui.button_properties("choice_button")
resolves to gui/button/choice_hover_background.png, and that image *is* the
blue bar the hover assertions look for. Delete it and choicetest fails on
"hovering paints a blue bar", which reads as a bug in whatever else changed
most recently rather than as a missing file.

The two binary fixtures are generated, with the generator committed beside
the output so the repo needs no encoder to run the suite:

  story/audio/one.mp3, two.mp3   tools/mkfixaudio.py
  choice/images/room.png         tools/mkfixtureimg.py -m room

Read mkfixaudio.py's docstring before shortening those tracks. They are 90
seconds for a reason: script.rpy plays them with `loop`, chan_rewind zeroes
out_frames on every loop (audio.c:526), and rbmusictest waits for the
position to *reach* a target -- so a track that wraps mid-test sends the
counter it is watching backwards, and the test does not fail cleanly.

convtest takes nothing either, and goes further than the rest: it writes a
one-entry MRPK archive and mounts that. A string catalogue is found by
enumeration, and mr_vfs_enum only enumerates an archive on this host -- a
loose tl/ directory in a fixture is invisible to a gcc build. Writing the
archive is also what keeps it independent of which game is installed, which
is the trap tltest documents.

cputest takes nothing either and is the smallest of them: it links src/cpu.c
alone and drives MR_CPU_DECODE with synthesised CPUID leaf values. Read its
closing note before trusting it for more than that -- the assembly that feeds
the decode on the target is not built here, and it deliberately does not
assert that this host has MMX, because that assertion could not fail.
probe/cpuchk.c is the half that runs on a processor.

standintest and cuttest are the two halves of the cutscene, split by what
each can be evidence about. cuttest takes a real game's pak and is about the
*build convention*: `wmake data` turning a video into audio/<name>.mp3 and
images/<name>.png, the script finding them by those names, and the wait
lasting the track's length. It could not use a fixture, because a fixture
cannot be evidence about a convention it was written to satisfy.

standintest takes the fixture pak and is about what the engine does with
those two assets once it has them: where the held frame lands on the stage,
what is drawn over it, and what is on the stage after the cutscene ends. It could not use a game,
and for the mirror-image reason -- the only cutscene either shipped game has
is Miwa's intro.webm, whose poster is 1280x720, so the stage-sized case is
the identity and both bugs it covers are invisible. Its fixture poster is
640x320 on purpose, and the test asserts that before anything else, so
regenerating it stage-sized fails loudly instead of going quiet.

The duration is cuttest's and is deliberately not standintest's: the host
waveOut shim consumes buffers the instant they are queued, so a stand-in
timed off its soundtrack here would be timed off a lie. standintest's
fixture label has no soundtrack and takes the fixed-length branch.

cutskiptest is a third, and the split is the same kind: it is about *which
exit* from the cutscene is taken, not about the picture or the duration. A
cutscene's wait can end five ways, and skipping -- Ctrl held, Tab toggled --
is the one that happens below both of mr_interp_update's teardown calls and
above run_until_wait, so the statement after the cutscene used to run with the
cutscene still up. Mid-story that is a frame of stale video; after a
splashscreen the statement is `return`, the story ends in the main menu, and
the teardown arriving a frame later restored the pre-cutscene scene over the
menu and took the main_menu screen out of the scene with it. The engine stuck
there with nothing on screen to click.

So its fixture label (fixture_splash) is a cutscene followed immediately by a
top-level `return` and nothing else, and the key assertions are about order
within a single update: the poster is off the display list *and* the main menu
is up after one frame, and the menu is still up ten frames later with Ctrl
still held. "The cutscene ended" passes on the broken engine -- the skip did
end it -- which is why that check is in the test but is not what it is for.
standintest cannot see any of this: its label has a say after the cutscene, so
a late teardown has a spare frame to arrive in and every claim it makes comes
out green either way.

placetest, wavtest, flagtest, enumtest, vbetest, packtest and scaletest take
nothing: the
first parses a throwaway script, wavtest and flagtest build the files they
read, and the last two are pure arithmetic over buffers they make themselves.
wavtest treats its first argument as the directory to write them in, so
handing it the pak fails with "cannot write <pak>/good.wav" rather than
anything about audio.

texttest takes the game directory, like most of them, but it loads
fonts/font*.dat and those are format v2 -- run `wmake fonts` before it, or it
stops with a note saying so rather than failing a screenful of comparisons.

These do not take the SRC list above, either because they stub out parts of
the engine themselves (linking the real ones is a duplicate-symbol error) or
because they need far less of it:

  audiostoptest  src/audio.c src/vfs.c src/mp3impl.c
  enumtest       src/vfs.c  (no arguments; it needs no game data)
  scaletest      src/scale.c src/gfx.c  (no arguments)
  vbetest        src/vbe.c src/vbedos.c (no arguments)
  packtest       src/pack.c src/gfx.c   (no arguments)
  wavtest        src/audio.c src/vfs.c src/mp3impl.c
  durtest        src/audio.c src/vfs.c src/mp3impl.c
  latencytest    src/audio.c src/vfs.c src/mp3impl.c  (+ -DHOSTSHIM_HOLD)
  texttest       src/font.c src/vfs.c src/gfx.c
  flagtest       expr list persist vfs pyfn md5 lex serial
  convtest       expr list persist vfs pyfn md5 lex serial tl (no arguments)
  tltest         parse lex expr vfs style slang slparse tl md5 pyfn list
  placetest      expr scene vfs lex parse style slang slparse image png gfx
                 mp3impl tl md5 persist pyfn serial list

blurtest is the opposite case: it takes the whole SRC list *plus* one file,
tools/harness/blurref.c, which is Ren'Py's blur serving as its oracle. That
file is MIT-licensed Ren'Py code with attribution in its header, it is test
scaffolding only, and nothing in src/ refers to it.

placetest's list is spelled out rather than described as "everything except
audio, ui, ..." because that description had drifted: it defines its own
mr_audio_poll so audio.c has to go, and musicroom.c then cannot come (it
calls mr_audio_current). rhythm.c is out for the same reason.

Every harness that links audio.c also links tools/hostshim/wavehost.c, and
run.sh adds it automatically when it sees audio.c in the source list. That
file is wave.h -- the seam between the mixer and the sound card, see
src/wave.h -- for a machine with no card: a device that consumes buffers
instantly, or holds them under -DHOSTSHIM_HOLD. It replaced the fake
mmsystem.h, which audio.c *included*; the two entry points below were
statics in whatever translation unit included them, and are ordinary
symbols now.

A harness that walks a story walks **tools/harness/story**, not the game.

That fixture is a short script -- a run of plain dialogue, a dissolve, a move,
`window hide`/`window show`, two music tracks and a stop, a title card held by
`$ renpy.pause()`, and a two-option menu -- laid out in the order the tests
look for those things. It symlinks the real gui.rpy, screens.rpy and art, so
the styles and the furniture are the real ones; only the story is fixed.

This was learned the hard way. These tests used to assert against the game's
own script.rpy, and the game is a work in progress: a chapter-select menu was
added one afternoon and five harnesses failed at once with a dozen alarming
messages, none of which meant anything. They were re-fitted, the script
changed again an hour later, and they failed again. A unit test that depends
on the current draft of the story is not testing the engine.

Two things the fixture does on purpose and should keep doing:

  - it declares its images with `image name = "..."` rather than relying on
    them being found in images/, because mr_vfs_enum only walks loose files
    on Windows and a host build discovers nothing by scanning;
  - it ends with `jump start` rather than `return`, so a harness that steps
    for a long time -- bandtest runs 1200 frames and then wants a line of
    dialogue to measure -- cannot run out of story and sit on an empty stage.

`answer_menu()` in those harnesses takes the first option of whatever menu is
up. The fixture has one, and so does the game; a harness that does not answer
it stops dead and every assertion about dialogue fails at once.

unlocktest still runs against the real game, on purpose: what it tests is that
game's persistent flags and the `build_rhythm_highscores()` in its screens.
flagtest covers the same engine behaviour with no game data at all.

A harness that renders anything must call mr_interp_start() before showing a
screen, as main.c does: that is where `define gui.* = ...` runs and where the
styles are finalised against those values. Skip it and the screens still lay
out, but every gui.* is None -- borders and padding quietly vanish, and the
layout dump looks plausible.

The shim's waveOutGetPosition reports a position that trails the bytes
written by HOSTSHIM_LAG_BYTES (a full NBUF-buffer queue -- keep it in step
with audio.c), because a real card does and the difference is the whole point
of mr_audio_output_position_ms. A shim that played instantly could not show
the bug it exists to prevent. Set the define to 0 for a device with no
latency.

hostshim_wave_sink is a function *pointer*, not a function, and the harness
defines it -- wavehost.c only declares it. Define it as
`void (*hostshim_wave_sink)(const short *, int) = my_sink;`. Declaring it as
a function links without complaint and crashes the moment audio is polled.

Add -fsanitize=address,undefined when hunting a crash. Run from a scratch
directory: they write and delete SAVE*.SAV and PERSIST.SAV there.

  afmtest   auto-forward, i.e. the quick menu's "Auto" button, which did
            nothing at all: the action stored the Preference's own name while
            the wait read Ren'Py's field name, afm_enable. The check that the
            button "reads back selected" is kept deliberately, next to the one
            that passes only when the real field moved -- a button agreeing
            with its own invented storage is what the bug looked like. Also
            the after-click rule, and a negative control running the same ten
            seconds of clock with Auto off. Then the two places where "does
            Auto apply here?" is answered by whether Ren'Py has a SayBehavior
            there: it walks the fixture to the menu on the clock alone, holds
            the menu for twenty seconds to show nothing picks for the player
            and that answering it does not switch Auto off, and crosses the
            title card's untimed `renpy.pause()`. Then a case per row of the
            table in NOTES, which is what the fixture's `afm_pauses` label is
            for: a timed pause, a hard one and an untimed one in a row, told
            apart by how long each lasts. Nothing else jumps to that label.
            It ends with two untimed *hard* pauses, which nothing the player
            presses can end -- so the cases there are clicking at one for
            3.3s, a save/load round trip inside it, and its two real exits,
            auto-forward and skipping. Two of them because each exit uses
            one up.
  substtest `[name]` substitution, in dialogue and in screen text, from the
            fixture's `interp_say` label. The substituted line is that
            label's *first* so the harness needs no navigation to reach it:
            an earlier version walked to it and silently measured a leftover
            line from `label start` instead. Also checks the typewriter
            counts the drawn text rather than the source, which is the half
            that fails invisibly.
  convtest  the `!t` conversion in a substitution: `text "[title!t]"`
            translates the *substituted value*, which nothing else can do.
            Text is translated as a template, before interpolation, in Ren'Py
            and here, so `text "[title]"` looks up the literal `[title]` and a
            variable's contents are never a key -- which is why a chapter
            title assigned under one language survives a switch, and why the
            flag exists. Both are asserted, the second deliberately: the
            un-flagged case is pinned as behaviour so the parity is written
            down. Also pins the flags still unimplemented (r, s, i, q, u, l,
            c), including that `t` applies inside a set such as `!tu` while
            the `u` is dropped
  prefixtest Python string prefixes -- u'', b'', r'', f'' and the pairs --
            which another game's gui.rpy uses for every colour it defines.
            Two halves: the literal parses with each prefix and r keeps its
            backslashes, and a define that uses one reaches the colour the
            *renderer* resolves for a hovered button. The second needs the
            colour named, not merely present: unparsed, the style inherits
            #66a3e0 from gui_button_text, so "is there a hover colour" and
            "does hover differ from idle" pass either way. Also guards
            against over-reading: `unlocked` and `rb` stay variables.
            Calls mr_interp_start, not just mr_interp_init -- the style
            expansion runs from start, and an empty style table answers every
            lookup with the caller's default, which looks exactly like the
            bug. It prints the "style: N styles" line for that reason.
  condtest  everything about a `menu:` body that is not a plain choice.
            Conditions (`"..." if expr:`), which are evaluated on arrival so
            the same menu can offer different options twice; a menu with
            every option conditioned away, which must be *skipped* rather
            than shown empty and unanswerable; config.menu_include_disabled,
            which keeps such an option on screen but unpickable; and both
            caption forms, a say menuitem and a bare narrated string.
            The fixture's conditional menu declares *three* options with the
            middle one conditioned away, so a row index and a declared index
            are different numbers and confusing them picks the wrong branch
            rather than drawing the wrong count. Its captioned menu gives
            each option several statements and a distinct line after the
            menu, because an off-by-one target lands on a real line either
            way and only naming the line tells them apart.
            Both labels open with a line of dialogue before the menu: without
            a checkpoint in front of it the menu is the oldest point and a
            rollback there has nowhere to go, which is indistinguishable from
            a rollback refusing to leave a menu.
            The repaint block is the one to read before touching
            mr_interp_redraw_kind. It asserts the engine *asks for frames*
            while a caption types, which is what was broken; the reveal is
            time-based, so the characters were always computed and an
            assertion about the text appearing cannot catch it. Mutating the
            fix back gives "1 of 16 frames asked for a repaint".
            It also drives step() the way the real loop does, reporting
            mr_interp_say_painted -- without that report the engine asks for
            frames forever and the block proves nothing.
            The press-to-skip block has the session's most instructive
            negative control: letting the ST_MENU branch fall through to the
            ordinary advance passed every check, because g_pc is still on the
            menu statement and re-executing it makes the menu heal itself.
            The checks that catch it are "nor restart the caption" and "nor
            repeat it in the history" -- the two things a re-run really does.
            Finally, the identifier block is the harness's only *external*
            oracle. Every other translation check compares our parser with
            our catalogue reader, so a digest that is wrong consistently
            passes; these two constants (chap1_939df797 with nointeract,
            chap1_851ede00 without) came out of a Ren'Py-generated
            tl/french/script.rpy. The "fixture carries the right id" check
            beside them stays green under the same mutation, which is the
            whole argument for having the oracle.
            It lives here and not in tltest on purpose -- see below.
  movetest  the move family: MoveTransition written longhand (which is just
            `move` with a non-default duration, and used to dissolve), the
            four vertical kinds, and a panned `scene` background.
            Read the fixture notes before adding to it. The pan phases exist
            in two shapes because one of them cannot see the bug: `move_bg`
            uses a 1280x720 backdrop, and for a backdrop no taller than the
            stage every candidate placement gives the same answer.
            `move_tallbg` uses tall.png -- 1280x1460 in two colour bands,
            from tools/mkfixtureimg.py -- so one pixel says which part of the
            backdrop is on screen. A *flat* tall image would be no good
            either: it covers the whole stage at both ends of the pan. The
            end position of that pan was wrong twice with everything else
            green, and this is why.
            Its step() composes the stage every frame, and into_transition()
            exists because of two traps: one mr_interp_advance does not get
            past a line of dialogue (it only completes the reveal), and
            mr_scene_busy must be tested *before* each advance or the harness
            skips the effect it is measuring. Both produced whole runs of
            failures that were the harness's own.
            There is deliberately no horizontal pixel trace: dark.png is not
            flat, so "first pixel unlike x=0" reads a constant whatever the
            engine does. That question was settled by logging render_move's
            own numbers instead; see NOTES.
  blurtest  im.Blur, and the only harness with a *verbatim Ren'Py* oracle:
            blurref.c is blur_filters/blur32_core/linblur32_core copied out
            of renpy-8.5.3-source/src/core.c, built alongside the engine, and
            run over the same pixels. The comparison is byte for byte, not to
            a tolerance. A reference written from the same paper would only
            prove the same mistake twice -- an almost-Gaussian is three box
            blurs whose widths come from a formula, and a wrong width or a
            wrong pass split still looks blurred.
            Its fixture is images/quad.png, four quadrants from
            tools/mkfixtureimg.py -m quad, because blurring a flat image
            returns the flat image: on room/street/dark/tall every check here
            would pass with the blur ripped out. There is a check that says
            exactly that, so the trap is recorded in the output.
            The three `image quad ...` declarations cover the grammar one
            radius at a time: one radius (circular), two radii (which a
            broken parser silently collapses to one), and a nested im.Flip.
            The flip one asserts that both manipulators ran, not their order
            -- a mirror and an odd-width box blur commute exactly, so
            swapping the two lines in image.c leaves this file green. That
            was checked, not assumed.
            It also prints the cost of a 1280x720 blur, which is not an
            assertion but is the number to watch: a blurred background pays
            it on every cache miss, on top of the PNG decode.
  animtest  `image <name>:` animations -- the block form, where a name
            resolves to a different frame as the clock moves. Three claims,
            which fail differently: the parse (frame count, which pause
            belongs to which frame, repeat, the per-frame transition), the
            resolution (mr_image_get returns the frame the clock says,
            wrapping or holding at the end), and the blend.
            Fixtures are images/anim{0,1,2}.png, 64x64 RGBA from
            tools/mkfixtureimg.py -m vband. Each is a left half and a right
            half, and the split is load-bearing: the left halves are opaque
            and differ in colour, so "which frame is showing" is one pixel
            read, while the right halves differ in *alpha*, which is the only
            way the premultiplied blend is observable. Two opaque frames mix
            identically whether the alpha handling is right or not, so an
            all-opaque fixture would pass with the premultiply ripped out.
            That is the same unobservability trap as the backdrop pan and
            then im.Blur, which is why mkfixtureimg.py grew alpha for this.
            The frame durations are unequal (100/200/400ms) on purpose: with
            equal ones, an implementation that divided elapsed time by the
            frame count instead of walking the frames would land on the right
            frame every time. There is a check asserting they are unequal.
            The blend is compared against a floating-point reference written
            from the definition, *not* transcribed from the integer code --
            that is what caught a premultiply scaled wrong by a factor of
            255, which still looks like a cross-fade. Exact values are also
            pinned by hand so the tolerance-of-1 sweep is not carrying the
            whole check, and there is a straight-mix reference alongside so a
            pass cannot be a pass on pixels where right and wrong agree.
            Nine deliberate breaks were tried against this file; eight went
            red. The one that did not is mr_image_set_time in main.c's
            frame(), which no harness links. See NOTES.
  placetest where a `show ... at X:` block puts an image. The integer/float
            rule, and -- since this is where the mouths bug lived -- the rule
            that a placement which says anything at all gets *no* default, so
            an axis it does not mention is zero rather than bottom-centre.
            That had no coverage even though the script already contained a
            y-only block; nothing looked at its x.
  branchtest menus in the rollback log, in both directions: rolling back
            stops *at* a menu and offers it again, one more press leaves it,
            and rolling forward answers it with the option recorded there.
            Also re-deciding a choice, and what that must do to the forward log.
            The last block does the whole menu-rollback sequence again
            *across a save*, because the live log and the one restored from a
            slot are separate code: the checkpoint's kind was never written
            to the file, so a loaded game thought every checkpoint was a say
            and rolling back over a menu ate a line out of the history
            screen. The in-memory block above had been green throughout. Its
            menu must stay uncaptioned -- a captioned one writes a history
            line and so pops correctly either way.
            The fixture's menu converges -- both options jump to the same
            label, one setting a flag -- which is the shape that defeats the
            "did the statement change?" test rollforward uses. Takes the
            branch that *writes* the flag first, on purpose: abandoning a
            branch that wrote nothing has no delta to restore and would pass
            whatever the logic did.
  seamtest  features *in combination*, which is where this engine's bugs
            have actually been. It tests nothing on its own: every block
            crosses two or more things in the order a player would. Written
            after three bugs in one week sat in seams between well-tested
            parts -- the game menu reappearing on a rollback, but only after
            a language switch; rollforward stopping after one step; the
            rhythm quit button swallowed by the give-up dialog. The
            invariant most blocks come back to is that an excursion into the
            interface leaves no trace in the story.
            Asks the scene for menus *by name*: the quick menu is a screen
            too, so counting reads 1 with nothing open and 1 with the menu
            up. Three negative controls are recorded in NOTES.
  rbtest    rollback, history, and rollback after loading a save
  menutest  opening and closing the game menu must not advance the story
  wintest   rolling back from a title card, where `window hide` is in force
  helptest  the help screen's Clavier/Souris tabs (screen variables), and
            the `key "game_menu"` binding: Escape leaves a sub-menu opened
            from the main menu, and in game the same key still closes the
            menu onto the story instead. Both directions, because reading
            the binding without honouring its `if main_menu` would send the
            in-game right-click to the main menu. Note "Lecture is on
            screen" does not distinguish them -- the navigation column is
            drawn on the sub-screen too -- so the check is that Preferences
            went away
  savetest  the save file round trip
  savatltest a save taken part way through a transform block, which used to
            load frozen -- the credits roll stopped where the save was taken
            and `fin` landed over a half-scrolled list. Takes the story
            fixture, label save_atl. Four shapes: one long step, two steps
            saved during the second (so the step index matters), `repeat`,
            and a block that had already finished and must not come back as a
            running one. Every assertion that counts is taken mid-flight: the
            final positions are the same whether the block resumed or
            restarted, so they pass against the bug. Last section covers the
            other half: a rollback (and a rollforward) after a load, onto a
            line an animation played over, which the rollback log used to
            restore frozen at the start of the block
  savmenutest a save taken at a choice menu, which used to come back with the
            right thumbnail and no menu: the player had to click, press
            space or roll the wheel before the options appeared. Takes the
            story fixture, label save_menu, which holds three menus
            differing only in what dialogue is up when they open -- none,
            an undismissed line, and the menu's own caption. Only the
            captioned one actually reproduced, which is the shape it was
            reported in. Every assertion is made with no input at all after
            the load, since an advance is exactly what used to fix it. Last
            two sections are the second half of the bug: the re-executed
            menu's checkpoint was appended past the one the file already
            held, so the log had the menu twice and the first Page Up after
            loading landed on the duplicate -- the same menu again. The
            no-save baseline runs first, because the assertion is that the
            two agree
  pausebacktest rolling back *to* a chapter title card. The card is
            `show screen title with fade` then `$ renpy.pause()`, and the
            pause took no checkpoint, so the card was the one thing in a
            script that rollback could not reach -- a step back from the line
            after it went straight past to the line before. Takes the story
            fixture, label pause_back, which puts a line and a variable on
            either side of a card and then offers both spellings of an untimed
            pause plus a timed one: Ren'Py checkpoints the untimed kind only
            (statementexports.py:151-154), so `pause` and `$ renpy.pause()`
            are stops and `pause 0.2` is not, and all three are asserted.
            Three separate faults, each with its own section: the missing
            checkpoint; the step back from the card itself, which landed on
            the card's own point and re-ran the pause, so the first Page Up
            appeared to do nothing (the menu bug's shape, in a pause); and the
            checkpoint screen list, which was never written to a save file at
            all -- so rolling back to a card in a loaded game drew its `scene
            black` and nothing over it. The last is why the test rolls back to
            a card twice, once live and once from a slot: the live log holds
            the screens and only the trip through the file lost them.
            Later sections cover a fourth fault found from a player's log.txt:
            two rollbacks delivered inside one frame -- key auto-repeat -- ran
            against a half-finished log, so the point stepped over was not
            recognised and nothing was captured for the way forward. Rolling
            back still worked; rolling forward moved the pc and left the
            variables behind, which on a card is another chapter's title or
            none. Pressed two and three times with no update between, both
            directions, plus an advance and a rollback in one frame. The two
            card titles are 3 and 40 characters so that lit pixels on the
            stage distinguish the right title, the other one, and black
  uitest    save thumbnail timing: the scene, not the menu
  clicktest clicking a save slot through the real focus list
  persisttest persistent data: survives a restart, a load and a rollback,
            and is not carried by a save slot
  confirmtest the confirm dialog: drawn over the screen that opened it,
            modal, `action yes_action` runs what Confirm was handed, and the
            nine-slice Frame background is really on the stage
  restarttest main menu then Start again: no stale line over the opening
            fade, no inherited rollback log, history or story variables
  bandtest  partial frames: while a line types out only the dialogue box is
            repainted, so every such frame is compared pixel for pixel
            against what a full frame would have drawn, over 1200 frames of
            real play. On a divergence it writes both stages to
            /tmp/band_*.ppm and prints the run-up of frames
  windowtest the dialogue window: `window show`/`window hide`, a line
            showing it whatever they left behind, and -- the part that took
            two wrong attempts -- an effect never touching it. Measures the
            box against the scene rendered without it, which isolates the
            window exactly, across a dissolve and across a move
  bartest   a bar takes its style from its *value*, not from the keyword, so
            `bar value Preference(...)` is a slider and gets xsize 350 while
            the jukebox's AudioPositionValue stays a plain bar and fills its
            viewport. Both halves matter: the obvious wrong fix is a default
            width on bars, which passes the first and breaks the second.
            Reads the styles out of the layout dump, and note the thickness
            check only looks right by luck -- gui.bar_size and
            gui.slider_size are both 25, so the width is the whole tell
  creditstest a dissolve must start from what is on screen. Runs the credit
            roll twice, once undisturbed and once with the game menu opened
            and closed mid-scroll, and asserts the first frame of the
            closing dissolve is *identical* to the frame before it. Exact,
            not a tolerance: one of the three bugs it caught was worth only
            6400 pixels (one frame of the roll's motion) and any sane
            tolerance would have passed it. Needs its own fixture because
            nothing else has a transform still running when a transition
            starts
  stalltest the screen must be right on every frame, *including* the ones
            the loop skips. bandtest cannot see a wrongly-skipped frame: it
            applies the same skip decision to its reference, so both go
            stale together and agree. This keeps the stage as the engine
            would have left it and compares against a full render taken
            every frame. It caught the last glyph of a line going unpainted.
            Two of its own traps are in its header comment -- invalidate
            after advance, and never render the reference to mr_stage().
            Seven walks, because the staleness depends where a frame falls
            in a line: reverted, some cadences pass and others fail, so one
            walk would be a coin flip. About 40s, the slowest harness --
            it renders the stage twice per frame
  quicktest the quick menu and the save/load page row: the overlay coming and going with the menus,
            the script's `config.overlay_screens.append` actually running,
            `$ quick_menu = False` emptying it, the greying of Retour and
            Chargement R., QuickSave/QuickLoad/Notify, Skip() and
            auto-forward. The load-bearing one is "a typing line still asks
            for a partial frame": the menu is up for the whole game, and a
            screen on the scene used to force a full 1280x720 recomposite
            per character. Two traps in it -- the layout dump is only
            written while the *live* list composites, so it is empty
            mid-transition (settle() first), and the redraw kind must be
            read on the frame *after* the say statement, since any frame
            that executed something is full by definition
  skiptest  skipping and the three "Avance rapide" preferences: Ctrl/Tab,
            stopping at unread text, stopping at a menu, the seen set
            round-tripping through PERSIST.SAV, and transitions suppressed
            both by the preference and by skipping. Writes and removes
            PERSIST.SAV, so run it from a scratch directory. Two traps
            recorded in its comments: it must restart with `Start()` and not
            mr_interp_start (the latter leaves the scene in place, so the
            opening dissolve has nothing to do and the transition check
            passes against a build that ignores the preference), and the
            frame counts are 32 vs 1 rather than 32 vs 0 because Start()
            itself costs a frame. The reset_persistent case drives both
            progress=False and progress=True on purpose: asserting one
            outcome cannot tell "honours the script" from "hardcoded", since
            a hardcoded 1 passes the True case and a hardcoded 0 the False
            one. It reads the flag out of the fixture's screens.rpy and
            asserts agreement, so editing that script moves the expectation
            rather than breaking the test
  dismisstest `dismiss action X` on the high scores screen: a click on empty
            space runs it, a click on Retour does *not* (dismiss must be the
            lowest priority or it eats the navigation), and a screen without
            one does not grow one. Also that screen's `key "K_SPACE"`, via
            mr_interp_screen_key -- main.c's WM_KEYDOWN cannot be reached
            from a harness, so only the lookup and the action are covered.
            Note "Meilleurs scores" cannot tell the two screens apart: it is
            the title of one and a tile label on the other
  preftest  the preferences radio and check buttons showing which option is
            chosen: Preference() and InvertSelected() answering selected, the
