  titlesavetest saving on a chapter title card. Two faults stacked here and
            the second was hidden by the first: the screen layer was cleared
            wholesale on load, which dropped `show screen title` along with
            the save menu; and the `$ renpy.pause()` holding the card was not
            saved either, so once the card came back the restored state ran
            straight on through `hide screen title` into the next scene. The
            test waits several seconds after loading and looks again, which
            is what separates the two
  memreport NOT a test -- a heap report, and deliberately outside the default
            run (it needs real saves in the working directory and always
            exits 0). Intercepts every allocation with -Wl,--wrap and
            attributes blocks to two stack frames, which is what separates
            the session-long stage surfaces from cached images. Writes its
            own saves now, on page 7, from two points 170 statements apart,
            and deletes them -- it used to need SAVEP1S*.SAV copied into the
            working directory and silently skipped the whole load half
            without them. Also prints cache occupancy per step and the
            freed/returned figures for an mr_image_flush, which is what says
            whether flushing on load would save anything; see NOTES.md.
            Takes an optional third argument: a statement budget, or `full`
            to walk the whole script (about 90s on Miwa, versus 4s for the
            400-statement default -- so the suite gets the short one). Only
            the full walk saturates the image cache, and a short walk's
            figures led to the opposite conclusion about flushing; quote the
            full one.
            Does not open the save or preferences screens, so the thumbnail,
            style and layout cache figures are floors.
  latencytest how long after being started a sound is actually audible. One
            waveOut stream means the mixer queue is the latency, and at the
            default depth that is the whole runway, over a second, against
            a 300ms hit window. Built
            with -DHOSTSHIM_HOLD so the shim's device keeps its buffers;
            without that nothing is ever outstanding and this measures 0
            whatever the engine does.
  durtest   every MP3's reported length against ffmpeg's, 44.1k stereo and
            48k joint stereo, CBR and VBR of each, plus a mono file. The
            length used to come from the first frame's bitrate, which doubled
            it for the nine VBR files -- and rhythm.c ends a run by it.
            Figures in the table are ffprobe's, not the engine's.
  audiostoptest `stop music fadeout` then `play music` of the same track,
            which is what fast-skipping through a chapter break does. Takes
            the pak and the game dir; links only audio.c and the VFS.
            Also that if_changed does not reopen a track already playing --
            asserted by position, since a reopen restarts the decode at
            zero. Note the loop flag in that call is the one being
            *requested*: pass 1 and the bug it guards against does not show,
            because the old condition only fell through when 0 was asked for,
            which is what a rollback asks after a checkpoint recorded a
            channel mid-fadeout
  placetest `yalign 1` vs `yalign 1.0`: integer positions are pixels. Parses
            a throwaway script, so it needs no game directory
  langtest  switching language changes the line on screen, the menus and the
            saved state, and drops the rollback log
  oldsavetest
            version 5, 6 and 7 slots still load, against real files kept in
            fixtures/. Takes the fixture pak, the fixture game dir, and the
            three save fixtures. The saves stay real -- a synthesised old
            save would only prove the reader agrees with the test's idea of
            the old layout, which is the thing in doubt -- and the twenty
            statements they name by id live in fixgame/script_game.rpy.
            Before that this needed *Miwa's* pak, and against any other game
            all three failed "it loads" while the variable section read
            fine: the desynced-save guard working as intended, presenting as
            a reader regression. It was chased as an intermittent for a
            while, because the pak path is the same file whichever game is
            built. The harness prints the engine's own log and a line saying
            so when it happens.
  tltest    every say statement finds its translation -- checks the identifier
            digest against the real tl files (parser + catalogue only).
            Runs on the fixture, whose twenty borrowed lines carry the ids
            *Ren'Py* computed for them -- so the parser is checked against
            Ren'Py and not against itself, which is the one thing a
            generated catalogue could not do. The invented lines beside
            them must all miss and fall through unchanged, which is the
            other half.
            It used to walk a real game and assert complete coverage of it,
            which made it a content audit: red on one untranslated line and
            red on a different game entirely. That belongs in the game's
            own build.
            Its coverage walk counts a captioned MENU statement as well as
            MR_ST_SAY: a menu's caption is translatable dialogue but rides on
            the MENU statement, so a say-only loop never looked at it -- which
            is how a caption's identifier could be wrong unnoticed. The
            fixture has no captioned menu, so that half is counted and not
            yet exercised.
  rgtest    the rhythm minigame: the beatmap's lanes and stride, PERFECT and
            GREAT scoring against the real windows, the combo multiplier,
            health and game over, the end-of-song summary and the high score.
            Also the top-right quit button, which was missing: that it is up
            only during the run proper, that hovering changes what is drawn,
            and that a click inside opens the dialog while one two pixels
            outside on any side does not. It reads the art's size from the
            image and recomputes only the .rpy's placement rule -- the first
            version hardcoded 48x48, the art is 56x56, and the only check
            that noticed was the one probing just outside the edge.
            Then the whole path through the interpreter -- `call screen
            rhythm_game(...)` out of script_game.rpy changing the display over
            and `num_hits, num_notes, rhythm_score = _return` unpacking to
            three different numbers. Ends by evaluating the extras menu's
            `build_rhythm_highscores()` -- a multi-line list literal, a
            conditional `.append` and a comprehension -- which is the only
            coverage the list support has.
            Also the give-up dialog: that it is framed rather than bare text
            (found by looking for the frame art's blue border along the
            middle row), that Oui and Non are clickable where they are drawn
            -- drawing and hit-testing now come from one layout, and a drift
            between them is invisible until a click lands nowhere -- that it
            appears during the countdown as well as during play, that it is
            translated and the box is measured on the translation, and that
            the music volume comes back afterwards whichever way the run
            ends. That last one is the interesting assertion: it checks the
            slider's value (0.6) rather than "not silent", because putting
            1.0 back passes a silence test and still throws the setting away.
            MR_DUMP=1 writes both frames to /tmp/rg_pause*.ppm to look at.
            The first half hands the module an audio path that cannot be
            opened, on purpose: no track starts, so the clock stays the one
            the test is stepping and a 200ms scoring window can be asserted
            on. One section does the opposite -- the real MP3 through the real
            mixer -- and asserts the song clock never runs backwards, which
            needs the shim's device-lag model below. Note the natural-end case runs at stride 200: at the real
            stride, skipping to the end of the song misses 488 notes and ends
            the run as a game over instead, which is correct but is a
            different path
  choicetest `menu:` -- the seven-option chapter select. Takes a fixture
            directory, tools/harness/choice, which symlinks the real game's
            gui.rpy, screens.rpy and art so the styles and the button images
            are the real ones, and has a script.rpy of nothing but the menu.
            Checks the options and their order, that each option's block runs
            (`$ chap7=True` then `jump chap7`, in that order, then the `if`
            after it), and the drawing: the buttons are gui.choice_button_width
            wide and gui.choice_spacing apart and the block is centred where
            choice_vbox says -- asserted against those variables rather than
            against numbers, so it still means something for another game.
            Hover and hit testing too, since both are this engine's own code.
            MR_DUMP=1 writes /tmp/choice.ppm and /tmp/choice_hover.ppm.
            Note the backdrop is black in those dumps on a host build:
            mr_vfs_enum only walks loose files on Windows, so `scene` finds
            no image. The menu itself is unaffected.
  texttest  the {b}, {i} and {u} tags: that the four baked variants are four
            different faces rather than aliases of the regular one, that the
            tags are counted per flag so the closes may come out of order,
            that a style survives a wrap, that a reveal of n characters draws
            exactly what the text cut to n characters draws, and that CJK --
            which has one weight -- falls back instead of vanishing.
            Comparisons are pixel hashes, because a variant quietly falling
            back to the regular glyph is the failure that would otherwise
            look like working code. Note the obvious version of the reveal
            test, comparing a pixel prefix against the settled line, is wrong:
            an italic glyph leans out past its own advance
  flagtest  one `persistent.<name>` is independent of every other: the
            variable store, the persistent file round trip, `== True`, and
            names that share a prefix. Needs no game data
  unlocktest the same thing driven by the real script, and the case that
            actually bit: clear the persistent data, start a new game, and
            the unlocks must not come back out of the baseline. It only
            reproduces on the run *after* a reset, so the test restarts the
            interpreter in the middle -- a single-run version of it passes
            against the bug
  scaletest the four scalers in scale.c, which were split out of present.c
            precisely so they could be reached: present.c is HDCs and DIBs
            and is not in the source list above, so its one piece of pure
            arithmetic had no coverage. Checks each against a double
            reference written from the filter's definition, and every
            one-row band against the full frame bit for bit -- the latter is
            what catches a bilinear seam at a band edge, which bandtest
            cannot see because it works at the stage level. Also pins that
            bilinear=1 is bit-identical at 1:1 and 2:1, where it declines.
            Four negative controls are recorded in NOTES.
  packtest  pack.c: a 32bpp ARGB surface to whatever the display wants. The
            other half of what was stuck inside present.c, and split out for
            the DOS presenter, which needs 8, 16, 24 and 32bpp over a linear
            framebuffer where the Win32 one needed only two. Checks each
            depth against a reference written from the field positions
            (rpos/rsize and the rest, straight out of the VBE ModeInfoBlock),
            the specialised loops against the generic one -- reached by
            describing a layout the fast paths refuse, as a card with blue at
            the top of the word would -- every band against the whole frame,
            and that nothing is written into the row padding, which is real
            at both ends: a DIB row is DWORD aligned and a VBE scanline is
            whatever the card says.
            Also mr_pack_snap_rect, which grows a rectangle out to the 4x4
            dither grid before it is packed on its own. That is what lets
            the DOS presenter repaint the few hundred pixels under the
            mouse pointer without leaving a square of wrong-phase dither
            trailing behind it. The check that matters packs a snapped
            sub-rectangle through a view into the same pixels and demands
            the bytes match the whole frame -- with the unaligned case
            asserted to *differ*, so the aligned one cannot pass
            vacuously.
            The band check is the one that matters: the 8bpp dither reads
            the absolute row index, so renumbering rows inside a band shifts
            the 4x4 phase and leaves a seam across the dialogue box while a
            line types out. Negative controls in NOTES.
            Two behaviours are pinned rather than endorsed. The fourth byte
            of a 32bpp pixel is whatever the surface held, because the fast
            path is a memcpy and nothing reads that byte. And pure white at
            8bpp dithers between the top two grey entries instead of being
            one index -- an ordered dither that offsets before quantising
            cannot reach the top step in every phase. Both are worth another
            look when there is a real 8bpp DOS screen to judge.
  inputtest what a key or a click *means* -- input.c. For the life of the
            project this was main.c's window procedure and could not be
            linked here at all; the platform seam (src/plat.h) moved it to a
            file that knows nothing about messages, and this drives it.
            Covers the fit (output pixel to stage pixel, and the letterbox
            rejection, now mr_fit_* in scale.c), the click dispatch order,
            the 'r' and 'f' hotkeys being claimed ahead of everything, a
            screen's own `key` beating the default behaviour, Page
            Up/Down/Backspace/Tab/Ctrl/Escape/the digits, and the wheel.
            The one worth the whole file is "a click consumed by a button
            does not cancel a skip": that ordering is what made the quick
            menu's "Avance rapide" impossible to switch off, and it was
            previously checked only by eye and mirrored into skiptest's own
            helper, which could drift from the real thing.
            Three negative controls are recorded in NOTES.
            Not covered: mr_plat_capture_mouse and mr_plat_restore_size,
            because plathost.c's versions do nothing. The 'r' checks assert
            the property that matters without them -- that nothing
            downstream sees the key.
            Two things about writing more of these. A harness must *draw*
            every frame, not just update: the focus list and the screen-key
            list are rebuilt during drawing, so an update-only loop aims
            clicks and keys at a stale frame. And it must wait for a line to
            finish typing before comparing, since a click on a half-typed
            line completes it rather than advancing -- both of those cost
            an afternoon here and produced convincing false failures.
  vbetest   the VBE mode picker: mr_vbe_decode, which turns one raw 256-byte
            ModeInfoBlock from int 10h/4F01h into a usable mode or refuses
            it, and mr_vbe_pick, which chooses between them. Every mode in
            it is a block built field by field the way a BIOS fills one in,
            and the card tables are the mode lists real hardware reports --
            the standard VBE 2.0 set, which has no 16:9 in it at all, and
            the same card with widescreen modes patched into the OEM range.
            Covers the refusals (banked-only, text, mono, planar, VBE 1.2
            with no PhysBasePtr, a sub-byte pixel, a pitch too small for a
            row) and the two firmware quirks: a blank RsvdMaskSize on a mode
            that is really 32bpp, which must not shrink the pixel, and VBE
            3.0's separate linear pitch, which differs from the banked one
            on cards that pad differently and shears the picture if the
            wrong one is used.
            The policy is five lexicographic keys and each has a check:
            truecolour outranks a larger palettised mode; the biggest
            *picture* wins rather than the biggest mode, so 1280x1024 beats
            1024x768 because the letterboxed 16:9 stage is bigger in it; a
            16:9 mode beats a 4:3 one showing the identical picture; depth
            chooses between equals; a duplicate resolves to the lower number.
            This is the part of the DOS port that cannot be debugged on the
            machine -- a wrong answer is a black screen with no log on it --
            which is why the judgement lives in a pure file and the int 10h
            code is next door in vbedos.c with none of it.
            Three negative controls are recorded in NOTES.
            vbedos.c is linked but not driven: it compiles to do-nothing
            functions off DOS, and linking it is what catches a prototype
            drifting, since no makefile here builds the DOS half.
            This harness also carries the DOS syntax-only pass, over
            vbedos.c and presdos.c, against the shim in tools/hostshim/dos.
            That pass finds typos and nothing else -- the shim's
            declarations come from the same reading of Watcom's API as the
            code, so it agrees with any misunderstanding.
  enumtest  mr_vfs_cat and mr_vfs_join, the bounded builders that replaced
            two unbounded sprintfs in vfs.c. Neither bug was reachable from
            here -- one is inside #ifdef _WIN32, the other needs loose mode --
            which is exactly why the helpers are public rather than static,
            and why the suite ran green past both for years. The target buffer
            sits between 0xAA fences so a write past the end is caught at the
            write. Restoring the old sprintf turns 6 of 22 checks red.
            Also pins the case the engine depends on: the longest legal loose
            directory plus the longest legal VFS name (191 + 1 + 255) fits
            MR_FSPATH_MAX.
  wavtest   the RIFF/WAVE reader, including two files built to be hostile: a
            data chunk longer than the file, and a chunk length with the top
            bit set. The second is a target-only crash -- `long` is 32 bits
            under Watcom, so the length reads back negative, the chunk walk
            steps backwards and memcmp reads far outside the buffer. On a
            64-bit host the same bytes stay positive and nothing happens,
            which is exactly why it is pinned down here

For the pak-first harnesses listed above:

  /tmp/langtest mrpy.pak "$PWD/tools/harness/fixgame"   # the pak is the game

A loose mount would find no translations at all: the catalogue is located by
enumerating tl/<lang>/, and mr_vfs_enum only walks loose files on Windows.
